---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="About · Sara Johnson"
  description="A design degree, a mainframe, and a laser cutter. How Sara Johnson got here and how she works."
>
  <header class="head">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      I got into software sideways, learned the unglamorous parts first, and have been getting
      useful in unfamiliar systems ever since.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Half art, half code</h2>

    <p>
      My degree is in Digital Media: Visual Language, which sounds unrelated to software until you
      notice that 3D animation is half art and half code. I came out of it writing HTML, CSS,
      JavaScript, ActionScript 3, Processing, and eventually Python.
    </p>

    <p>
      Then I started at Aon Hewitt, where none of that mattered. Nobody teaches JCL or SQL for IBM
      DB2 in a design program, and the job needed both, so I learned them from the ground up. When I
      moved up a few years later I moved onto a modern stack at the same time, and taught myself C#
      and ASP.NET MVC through trial by fire.
    </p>

    <h2>How I work</h2>

    <p>What engineers who've worked with me say:</p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>She uses the code as documentation.</p>
      <p>She checks in with us as humans.</p>
      <p>She always wants to dive into the details.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      The first one saves everybody time. The second one is what makes the third one tolerable.
    </p>

    <h2>Font &amp; Flame</h2>

    <p>
      I design vectors for lasercraft and fabricate them for a growing customer base. Nights and
      weekends, and I own all of it: financial planning, product launches, customer service, the
      lot. You can see it at <a href="https://fontandflame.com"
        >fontandflame.com</a
      >.
    </p>

    <p>There's no safety net, which turns out to be an efficient teacher.</p>

    <p>
      Further back, three years selling video games on the floor at GameStop: point of sale,
      inventory, trade-ins, customers. Operator-side experience of the thing being sold, and I
      think about it whenever I'm designing for somebody who has to use my product all day.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .head h1 {
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .prose {
    margin-top: var(--section-gap);
  }

  /* The three quotes are a list of separate voices, not one passage. */
  .prose blockquote p {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
</style>
